 examples of these naturally occurring  opioids are morphine and codeine  there's also semi-synthetic opioids so  these are substances that are chemically  related to the naturally occurring  opioids but they've been slightly  altered  examples of these would be hydromorphone  and oxycodone  and then another type of opioid are  known as synthetic opioids  now these still act on opioid receptors  in the body however chemically they have  entirely different chemical structures  so they're chemically unrelated to the  naturally occurring opioids but they  still act on the same receptors  which brings me to what is the opioid  crisis  so the opioid crisis is the issue that  has arisen  based on the use of opioids  and the increase in desks deaths and  hospitalizations that have arisen based  on  opioid misuse  three waves have been identified  so the first one began in the 1990s  and was related to the use of  prescription opioids  an example of that would be the  widespread use of oxycodone  and back in the 1990s  it was believed that um use of these  prescription opioids  were not going to cause addiction  problems however that did not turn out  to be the case and we saw an increase in  abuse and increase in overdoses and also  an increase in death  the second wave occurred in  around 2010  and this was an increase in the  occurrence of heroin overdoses and  deaths  and one of the issues that occurs when  people can become addicted to opioids  if they can no longer get prescription  opioids then some individuals will turn  to other sources  to find their opioids  and then the third wave relates to the  use of synthetic opioids  mainly the use of fentanyl and compounds  related to fentanyl  known as fentanyl analogues  and then interestingly recently  some researchers have started to  identify  what's been described as a possible  fourth wave of the opioid crisis  and this involves poly substance abuse  so this is the use of opioids and other  drugs um usually stimulant drugs  methamphetamine and cocaine are the two  most common stimulants that have been  identified as being part of this fourth  wave  so this graph comes from uh ontario in  canada uh this is where i work um the  province of ontario has a population of  just under 15 million and so the data  that comes from ontario is useful for  monitoring trends in drug use  this information comes from ontario  public health  and they use information about opioids  that have been detected  in death investigations and they publish  this information um  uh on the internet and it's available  for  [Music]  anybody to access  you can see from this graph quite  clearly uh the the issue of fentanyl  and so this is relates to the third wave  of the opioid epidemic  and before  2015 um there weren't that many uh  fentanyl-related deaths in the province  of ontario but since that time the  number of deaths that occur where  fentanyl has been detected has increased  every single year  and  you can see that the last year where the  data is available so that's 2020 there  were over 2 000 deaths where fentanyl  was present at death  you find that these drug trends  are also seen in the us  so canada and the us  often you definitely see similar trends  in drug use and that is the same for the  opioid epidemic  so for example  in around 73 of all opioid-involved  deaths in 2019  were synthetic opioids this data comes  from the cdc  in  2019 so that's  that accounts for about 36 000 deaths  that all involved synthetic opioids  interestingly this data also shows that  uh the fentanyl which is the uh  main drug that's seen in these deaths  it's more likely to be from illicitly  manufactured uh fentanyl um compared to  pharmaceutical fentanyl and this shows  that the shift that's occurred in the  opioid crisis um if you remember the  first wave mainly involved prescription  opioids but now the shift has definitely  changed to more illicit opioids  so what's the role of a forensic  toxicologist  so a forensic toxicologist uh plays a  very important role  in  the opioid crisis  because the role of forensic  toxicologists and toxicology labs is  going to be  looking for  drugs so the different opioids in  various cases so for example in death  investigations  which opioids are present  you will also want to know kind of how  much drug is present so you can  interpret the findings so not every  opioid detected at death  means that it contributed to the death  and a toxicologist can assist with that  kind of interpretation is it a  concentration high enough to cause death  for example  toxicologists can help by doing research  into the toxicity of opioids  and then  collaboration between toxicology  laboratories with law enforcement  deaf investigators so including coroners  medical examiners and also public health  officials can be very important  so i mentioned the significance of  fentanyl in the third wave of the opioid  crisis so i'm just gonna do a little bit  of an overview on uh fentanyl and one of  the major  fentanyl analogues that seen in forensic  casework  so fentanyl is a fast-acting synthetic  opioid  it's a very strong drug so it's a  hundred times stronger than morphine and  morphine itself is a very uh powerful  opioid drug  it's used clinically to pre-medicate  prior to surgery  it's also available via prescription so  people are suffering from chronic pain  conditions may be prescribed fentanyl  pain patches  these are patches that can be worn on  the skin to give a constant pain relief  for up to three days at a time  and then fentanyl is available like  during hospital treatment and it's  usually in the form of an injectable  liquid  i did mention though how the shift of  opioid use during the opioid crisis has  moved away from prescription to more  illicit forms of drugs so fentanyl and  now  is found on the streets in terms of it's  often sold as powder  sometimes it's sold as heroin so drug  users may not be aware of the exact drug  that they're buying  this can be significant because heroin  is an opioid but it's not as strong as  fentanyl so if a drug user thinks  they're buying a less powerful  drug it may lead to an accidental  overdose  another way that fentanyl is available  is  through the use of counterfeit  pharmaceuticals  these are drugs that are designed to  look like  actual pharmaceutical medications and  however they are made illicitly and  don't contain the the drugs that people  think it does uh the picture on this  slide shows some green tablets that are  designed to look like oxycontin tablets  oxycontin contains oxycodone which is an  opioid drug  but again it's a drug that's not as  powerful as fentanyl and there has been  a large number of overdose  overdoses and deaths that are occurred  when people have been using these  counterfeit pharmaceuticals  not realizing what drugs they were  taking  and then fentanyl analogues are also  being detected in forensic casework and  fentanyl analogues are chemical  substances that are closely related to  fentanyl  the one of the most common fentanyl  analogues that seen in forensic casework  in terms of overdose deaths is a drug  called car fentanyl  carfentanil first appeared in um kind of  on the streets in canada in around 2016.  and this mirrors when uh it was first uh  first started to occur in the united  states as well  um carfentanil is an opioid drug um it's  approved for use uh  in large animals so some vets may use  carfentanil but it's never been approved  for use in humans  it's an extremely potent opioid  so it's a hundred times stronger than  fentanyl  and approximately 10 000 times stronger  than morphine so  if you recall fentanyl itself is an  extremely potent drug but now we're  looking at a drug that is a hundred  times  stronger than fentanyl  so another thing that's important  in the opioid crisis is something called  novel psychoactive substances  now the definition of that is that they  are they legally mimic the effects of  traditional drugs of abuse so for  example hallucinogens  stimulants  cannabinoids or opioids however they're  not internationally controlled so  legally they can be described as being  kind of in a gray area uh their soul  kind of over the internet  but in terms of uh the opioid class of  novel psychoactive substances and some  of the issues occur with this is that  these new substances uh they can appear  and then sometimes they can disappear  rapidly so in in certain countries or  part like  certain geographical reason regions uh  maybe for a few months you might detect  this novel a psychoactive opioid um it  might cause overdoses it might cause  fatalities and then a few months later  it seems to disappear and you don't see  it again in case work  other times a new drug will appear and  then it remains popular and you and you  see it  for more than a few months  so laboratories need to be able to adapt  quickly  this can be hard because a lot of  toxicology laboratories have limited  resources  so sometimes you need to make decisions  well which drugs are more prevalent is  this a  potent more toxic opioid is it something  that we need to develop a method for  rapidly  an example of that would be  carfentanil which i talked about earlier  so this research  i was involved in with some of my  colleagues from the center of forensic  sciences in ontario and and what this  research looked at was to provide  information on when car fentanyl  appeared in canada how did the  laboratory respond to the appearance of  car fentanyl so we looked at  adding carfentanil to one of the  common methods of analysis that we use  so we were able to start screening and  quantitating carfentanil in all the  routine death investigations  and that enabled us to  help the deaf investigators and provide  important information  for public health too to monitor this  new  dangerous drug and to see what trends  were happening and it's really important  for forensic toxicologists to share the  information they have with the  scientific and also the medical  community  one of the  things from this study was that we  looked at what other drugs were present  in deaths where  carfentanil was significant  we found that the most common  co-occurring substance in  carfentanil-related deaths was cocaine  and all benzoligonine which is a  breakdown product of cocaine  we found  the  indications of cocaine use in 54  of cases  this is similar to other published  research that also found stimulant use  with carfentanyl  so for example a study from florida  showed that cocaine was the most  frequently detected drug with  carfentanil  and a study from alberta in canada  showed that in that area it was  methamphetamine that was the most  frequently detected drug in combination  with  carfentanil and this type of information  adds to the  growing evidence about the potential  fourth wave of the opioid crisis  if you recall earlier i was talking  about people now saying that um opioid  use  it seems to be now occurring more  frequently with  um  stimulant drugs so this carfentanil  information  adds to this  fourth wave  i did mention about the novel  psychoactive substances and how they can  appear and then sometimes disappear  so carfentanil when it appeared in  canada it's still a significant opioid  it's still seen in many debt  investigations so it's it's frequently  observed in many forensic cases  other drugs so for example u47700  was a is a novel a psychoactive opioid  it appeared around the same time as  carfentanil in canada  and also in the us  users apparently report that they get  more euphoria taking you four seven  seven zero zero compared to fentanyl so  it was one of the reasons why uh it it  was becoming popular  it was a drug that was developed  by a pharmaceutical company  way back in the 1970s but it was never  used in humans so there was limited  information about the toxicity of this  substance  they did change the legal status in 2017  it became a scheduled one controlled  substance  and it hasn't um  it hasn't been frequently detected in  forensic casework  in canada  in recent years so this is one of those  examples where it was useful to be able  to detect this drug  however the trend for this particular  drug seems to be the the use has  declined  in terms of sharing information with  public health um the ability to forensic  toxicology labs to add a new opioid to  an analytical method  can be very important so it can provide  important surveillance information for  public health agencies  sometimes timely data collection and an  outbreak can have a significant impact  in supporting public policy and it can  allow kind of interventions  allow harm reduction  to be put into place to try and reduce  the dangerous overdoses or even deaths  that occur following opioid use  also  toxicology labs can help contin uh  contribute to research  for example about at-risk populations  an example of the role that forensic  toxicology can can play in this is um  drug alerts  so some organizations will work with  toxicology laboratories to enable rapid  drug testing of street drugs  an example of this would be in toronto  their drug checking service so this is  something where drug users can actually  send a small portion of their drug um to  a lab and they will test it and let them  know what is actually in in the drug  it's quite common that people buying  drugs illicitly they may think that  they're buying one substance it might  not be that substance or it could be cut  with other dangerous chemicals so it's a  way of reducing harm  for this drug checking service all the  results are monitored and then alerts  can be issued to the general public  so for example  in the spring of 2021 the toronto's drug  checking service issued an alert to let  potential drug users know that car  fentanyl was being detected in some  drugs that were being sold as fentanyl  because carfentanil is so much stronger  than fentanyl alerting users that the  drugs that they were buying might  actually be a lot stronger can sometimes  help reduce the risk of overdose because  they're going to be more careful  hopefully  if they're consuming those drugs so it  plays an important part in harm  reduction and can alert drug users about  potential contaminants  these types of early warning systems  they don't just come from testing the  illicit drug supply  you can get data from toxicology  laboratories information from  overdoses like hospitalizations  and you can alert law enforcement you  can alert public health and it can be a  crucial part in helping to deal with the  opioid crisis  so an example could be there could be a  certain  geographical area where a number of  deaths occur over a short period of time  and some of the toxicological data that  arises from that might be an indication  that a certain drugs are present in an  area so for example maybe carfentanil is  present and it's being sold as either  fentanyl or heroin and if people are  alerted to this  again it may help prevent overdoses  because they're aware that there's  something more dangerous in the drug  supply  toxicology data  is also important for general research  into  different areas of the opioid crisis  they can help look at drug trends they  can look at vulnerable populations  a couple of examples of that  this is an example  of a study that was looking at  opioid-related deaths in ontario during  the covid19 pandemic  so uh the  toxicology data and death investigation  data was looked at for the first 15  weeks of the pandemic  and it was found that there was a 38  increase in opioid related deaths  some of the trends that were seen with  these deaths were the individuals were  using drugs alone um outdoors in hotels  and motels  um during this time there was a lockdown  in ontario and people were advised that  they shouldn't leave their homes um  unless they had essential reasons uh for  doing so and so there was the  possibility that you know people were  less likely to deal uh to seek  assistance with  any drug problems they were having  they so they didn't access treatment  they maybe had reduced support network  so for example people using drugs alone  if they do overdose they don't have  anybody to assist them  so this was some useful information that  toxicology data helped contribute to  some research that i've been involved in  recently has been about opioid use in  correctional facilities  so we know from previous research that  inmates are particularly high risk for  opioid overdoses in comparison to the  general population and so we wanted to  look at drug related deaths in the  ontario prison system  to see you know how has the opioid  crisis impacted this vulnerable  population did we see trends that  mirrored the general population and  could some of this information be used  for potential harm reduction measures  one of the things that we did see so we  we looked at data from a death that are  occurred over a 10-year period from 2009  to 2019  and  um  it did mirror the general population in  terms of seeing a significant increase  in fentanyl-related deaths  which was  expected  and again it was useful information to  provide kind of public health people  working in correctional facilities  and some information that that's come  out of research like this  and some inquests that have occurred  into deaths that are occurred in these  facilities is to try and provide the use  of naloxone kits  in these facilities  naloxone or narcan is an antidote  to for opioid overdoses and it can be  extremely effective at um  uh uh saving lives uh reducing overdose  deaths and so having this kind of data  from uh research into opioids that are  seen during death  can help  they can help change  public policy  so just to conclude what i've been  talking about um i think that it's  important to note that in terms of the  opioid crisis uh collaboration it's  essential for helping to prevent  overdose deaths and just general  overdoses the opioid crisis has been a  significant health concern  and forensic toxicologists can help  provide timely data and also some  comprehensive surveillance  and it's hoped that this can help inform  efforts to prevent and respond to  various opioid overdoses  so thank you very much for listening uh  if you have any questions please feel  free to contact me  you can use my email address  karen.woodall at newtoronto.ca  i'd be happy to correspond and answer  any questions that you have  so thank you for listening

