17  it became a scheduled one controlled  substance  and it hasn't um  it hasn't been frequently detected in  forensic casework  in canada  in recent years so this is one of those  examples where it was useful to be able  to detect this drug  however the trend for this particular  drug seems to be the the use has  declined  in terms of sharing information with  public health um the ability to forensic  toxicology labs to add a new opioid to  an analytical method  can be very important so it can provide  important surveillance information for  public health agencies  sometimes timely data collection and an  outbreak can have a significant impact  in supporting public policy and it can  allow kind of interventions  allow harm reduction  to be put into place to try and reduce  the dangerous overdoses or even deaths  that occur following opioid use  also  toxicology labs can help contin uh  contribute to research  for example about at-risk populations  an example of the role that forensic  toxicology can can play in this is um  drug alerts  so some organizations will work with  toxicology laboratories to enable rapid  drug testing of street drugs  an example of this would be in toronto  their drug checking service so this is  something where drug users can actually  send a small portion of their drug um to  a lab and they will test it and let them  know what is actually in in the drug  it's quite common that people buying  drugs illicitly they may think that  they're buying one substance it might  not be that substance or it could be cut  with other dangerous chemicals so it's a  way of reducing harm  for this drug checking service all the  results are monitored and then alerts  can be issued to the general public  so for example  in the spring of 2021 the toronto's drug  checking service issued an alert to let  potential drug users know that car  fentanyl was being detected in some  drugs that were being sold as fentanyl  because carfentanil is so much stronger  than fentanyl alerting users that the  drugs that they were buying might  actually be a lot stronger can sometimes  help reduce the risk of overdose because  they're going to be more careful  hopefully  if they're consuming those drugs so it  plays an important part in harm  reduction and can alert drug users about  potential contaminants  these types of early warning systems  they don't just come from testing the  illicit drug supply  you can get data from toxicology  laboratories information from  overdoses like hospitalizations  and you can alert law enforcement you  can alert public health and it can be a  crucial part in helping to deal with the  opioid crisis  so an example could be there could be a  certain  geographical area where a number of  deaths occur over a short period of time  and some of the toxicological data that  arises from that might be an indication  that a certain drugs are present in an  area so for example maybe carfentanil is  present and it's being sold as either  fentanyl or heroin and if people are  alerted to this  again it may help prevent overdoses  because they're aware that there's  something more dangerous in the drug  supply  toxicology data  is also important for general research  into  different areas of the opioid crisis  they can help look at drug trends they  can look at vulnerable populations  a couple of examples of that  this is an example  of a study that was looking at  opioid-related deaths in ontario during  the covid19 pandemic  so uh the  toxicology data and death investigation  data was looked at for the first 15  weeks of the pandemic  and it was found that there was a 38  increase in opioid related deaths  some of the trends that were seen with  these deaths were the individuals were  using drugs alone um outdoors in hotels  and motels  um during this time there was a lockdown  in ontario and people were advised that  they shouldn't leave their homes um  unless they had essential reasons uh for  doing so and so there was the  possibility that you know people were  less likely to deal uh to seek  assistance with  any drug problems they were having  they so they didn't access treatment  they maybe had reduced support network  so for example people using drugs alone  if they do overdose they don't have  anybody to assist them  so this was some useful information that  toxicology data helped contribute to  some research that i've been involved in  recently has been about opioid use in  correctional facilities  so we know from previous research that  inmates are particularly high risk for  opioid overdoses in comparison to the  general population and so we wanted to  look at drug related deaths in the  ontario prison system  to see you know how has the opioid  crisis impacted this vulnerable  population did we see trends that  mirrored the general population and  could some of this information be used  for potential harm reduction measures  one of the things that we did see so we  we looked at data from a death that are  occurred over a 10-year period from 2009  to 2019  and  um  it did mirror the general population in  terms of seeing a significant increase  in fentanyl-related deaths  which was  expected  and again it was useful information to  provide kind of public health people  working in correctional facilities  and some information that that's come  out of research like this  and some inquests that have occurred  into deaths that are occurred in these  facilities is to try and provide the use  of naloxone kits  in these facilities  naloxone or narcan is an antidote  to for opioid overdoses and it can be  extremely effective at um  uh uh saving lives uh reducing overdose  deaths and so having this kind of data  from uh research into opioids that are  seen during death  can help  they can help change  public policy  so just to conclude what i've been  talking about um i think that it's  important to note that in terms of the  opioid crisis uh collaboration it's  essential for helping to prevent  overdose deaths and just general  overdoses the opioid crisis has been a  significant health concern  and forensic toxicologists can help  provide timely data and also some  comprehensive surveillance  and it's hoped that this can help inform  efforts to prevent and respond to  various opioid overdoses  so thank you very much for listening uh  if you have any questions please feel  free to contact me  you can use my email address  karen.woodall at newtoronto.ca  i'd be happy to correspond and answer  any questions that you have  so thank you for listening
 

